{
  "version": 1,
  "provenance": {
    "sio": "The world selector wraps0..12 and indexes13 world-name and logo-ID entries. Thirteen32-byte native SioWorldEntry records end at the independently referenced12-entry cursor-offset table; difficulty selection uses1..11 plus the physical zero entry. The13x12 card-position region contains12 navigation slots and its native zero terminator record. This entire change-card mode and table are genuinely absent in EU: its old absolute gUnk_09EF150C alias incorrectly coincided with gWorldNames. No EU definition or bytes are fabricated. All C placements retain their actual regional extents and opaque resource pointers remain assets.",
    "world_intro": "Won intro loops10 sprites and wraps its affine-frame index0..19, proving two10-record layouts and20 scales. The Agr schedule has19 live records plus its native final zero record before the next TaskDesc. TtMotion retains its canonical two6-element arrays, including unused stored values. The line task allocates33 entries; the physical33x3 halfword table includes31 live tuples, one extra stored tuple and a final zero tuple. It ends before two separate zero alignment bytes. Bks creates every explicit object ID0..13, proving the14-entry sprite, coordinate and timing arrays; its palette cycle proves reads only at entries 1 through 6 of the paired byte sequences. The remaining halfword-table gaps are supplied by ordinary compiler alignment.",
    "wlogo_native_owner": "The world-logo callbacks repeatedly consume these movement, scheduling, coordinate and animation-selector tables, interleaved with their own TaskDesc records in the same regional order. The Pooh logo creates indexes 0 through 3 of its four-byte selector. Ordinary definitions in wlogo.c reproduce the complete 37-object, 1804-byte initialized-data run. The two palette sequences are reconstructed as eight-entry s8 arrays with the existing seven initializer values and an implicit final zero. Their earlier seven-byte extents were introduced by recovery commit c3afb221 from the maximum observed index, while predecessor declarations were unsized; no original symbol-size evidence established seven. All three ROMs contain the same paired eight-byte slices, including the two terminal zero bytes. Eight-entry capacity is an explicit reconstruction assumption, not a claim that index 7 is read or that the original authoring form is uniquely proven. All live entries, original addresses, code, relocations and RAM remain unchanged. The 23 lower-ROM names still interleave independent assets and retain their existing placements; this batch does not reconstruct that separate readonly boundary."
  },
